Note: teleop also routes through RawCmdVelCallback — if I add estop/bumper block in RawCmdVelCallback, teleop is also blocked during estop (correct: E-stop must block everything).

Manual takeover: gate doesn't block autonomous during manual takeover?? source_blocked doesn't include manual_takeover... The README claims hierarchy includes Manual Takeover. Where does manual takeover stop autonomous? Perhaps dispatcher/path_tracker stop sending when takeover (their concern). Not our current bug.

Fix design (TDD): add a pure function? The raw callback logic: 

```cpp
auto decision = gate_->Evaluate(cmd);
if (estop_active_ || auto_fault_latched_ || bumper_active_) {
  decision.linear... = 0; decision.allowed=false; severity critical; reason = "estop"/"bumper";
}
```

Best: extend ComputePeriodicPublish? No — different function. Add a small pure helper in safety_publish_decision.hpp: `ApplyLatchedBlocks(decision-ish)`. Or put the check into gate itself: extend `CmdVelGate` so source_blocked is consulted for all *registered latched sources*... Simpler and testable: add pure function `OverrideLatchedCritical(bool estop_or_fault, bool bumper, double& x, ...)`. 

Actually cleanest conceptual fix: estop/bumper events should refresh while active (Option C) AND/OR raw path latch check. Which is more correct?

Option C alone: WatchdogTick (100ms) re-pushes estop event while estop_active_ → event fresh within TTL always while held. After release, source_blocked false anyway. This makes gate's Evaluate path correct without touching raw callback, keeps single decision path. But relies on push-before-expiry ordering (100ms tick vs2000ms TTL — fine). Also bumper. Also auto_fault_latched_ — no event pushed for auto_fault ever (only estop/bumper/manual?). auto_fault_latched_ blocks via source_blocked("estop") only if an estop event exists unexpired... auto_fault_latched_ true with no estop event → gate never blocks raw path even within TTL! Wait — was that a pre-existing bug? Before TTL, if estop was pressed once (event in history forever) then released, auto_fault_latch could engage later — event still in history (never expires pre-TTL) → blocked. Pre-TTL behavior: estop event in history forever + source_blocked(estop)=auto_fault_latched_ → auto fault engages block via stale estop event. That's fragile but "worked". Post-TTL: auto_fault_latched_ has NO fresh event → gate doesn't block raw path at all → FaultSupervisor kEmergencyStop latch doesn't actually stop raw cmds (only periodic zero via in.estop_or_fault → periodic zeros, raw path passes non-zero → same race). So both estop held >2s and auto_fault_latched_ (without recent estop event) are broken in the raw path.

The robust fix: RawCmdVelCallback must apply the latched block flags directly (mirror of ComputePeriodicPublish's first branch). That covers estop, auto_fault, bumper regardless of event freshness — single point. Then gate TTL only handles transient watchdog events (its intended purpose). This is Option A.

Implementation:
```cpp
auto decision = gate_->Evaluate(cmd);
//锁存型安全源（estop/bumper/自动故障）不依赖事件 TTL：事件可能已过期，
// 但锁存状态仍要求阻断，直接覆写为零速 critical。
const char* latched_reason = estop_active_ || auto_fault_latched_ ? "estop" : bumper_active_ ? "bumper" : nullptr;
if (latched_reason) { decision.linear_x/y/angular=0; allowed=false; severity=kCritical; reason=latched_reason; }
